    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) In calculations, award a method mark for correct working even where the final answer is wrong. (4) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- Animal feed is the **food given to farm animals** (1 mark)\n- to keep them **alive, healthy and productive** - for growth, reproduction and the production of meat, milk and eggs (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Award 1 mark for ‘food given to farm animals’ alone. Do NOT accept ‘grass’ by itself.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) Cattle - **grass, forage, hay or silage**\n- (ii) Laying hen - **layer mash**\n- (iii) Pig - **pig grower mash or pellets** (accept kitchen and farm waste)\n- (iv) Day-old chick - **chick mash (starter mash)**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). The feed must suit the animal AND its age. Do NOT accept ‘layer mash’ for a day-old chick or ‘silage’ for a chick.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Proteins**\n- **Carbohydrates**\n- **Fats and oils**\n- **Mineral salts**\n- **Vitamins** (also accept **water** and **roughage/fibre**)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE correct classes (3 marks). Do NOT accept named feedstuffs such as ‘maize’ or ‘fish meal’ here - those are sources, not classes of nutrients.*\n\n### Question 2 (11 marks)\n\n**(a)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Carbohydrate** (55 %)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Accept ‘carbohydrate’ with or without the percentage.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- Protein = 22 % of 50 kg = (22 ÷ 100) x 50 (1 mark)\n- = **11 kg** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 method mark for the correct working and 1 mark for the answer with the unit. Award the method mark even if the arithmetic is wrong. A bare correct answer of 11 kg scores both marks.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Chicks are growing rapidly**, and protein is the nutrient that **builds body tissues, muscles and feathers** (1 mark).\n- **Adult birds have finished growing**, so they need mainly energy and maintenance feed and need a smaller proportion of protein (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The first mark is for linking protein to growth or body building; the second is for the comparison with adult birds.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- To check that the **proportions of nutrients suit the type and age** of his animals (for example starter mash for chicks).\n- To **compare the quality and price** of different brands, and to check the **net mass** and the **expiry (or manufacturing) date** so that stale feed is not bought.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO sound reasons (2 marks). This is the curriculum exemplar on selecting feed from the proportions shown on the label.*\n\n**(e)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Mineral salts** - calcium and phosphorus (1 mark)\n- used by a laying hen to form **strong egg shells** (also accept **strong bones**) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Accept ‘calcium’ for the nutrient mark. The egg-shell answer is the expected use for a laying hen.*\n\n**(f)** [2 marks]\n\n- To stop the feed from **taking up moisture and going mouldy** - mouldy feed can **poison the animals**.\n- To **keep out rats, weevils and other pests**, and to **preserve the nutrients** so that the feed stays fresh until it is used.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct reasons (2 marks).*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) Protein - **builds and repairs body tissues**, for growth, muscle and the production of milk and eggs\n- (ii) Carbohydrate - **supplies energy** for movement, work and warmth\n- (iii) Mineral salts - **form strong bones, teeth and egg shells** and keep the blood healthy\n- (iv) Vitamins - **protect the animal from diseases** and keep the body working properly\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). Accept any one cor"
