    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award a method mark for correct working even where the final answer is wrong, and insist on the per cent sign where a percentage is asked for. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed; medical terms spelt as they sound, such as 'gonoria' for gonorrhoea or 'meningitas' for meningitis, earn the mark.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **P - rod-shaped**, like short sticks (accept *bacillus*, rod-like, cylindrical) (1 mark).\n- **Q - round or spherical, joined together in pairs** (accept *coccus*, *cocci*, ball-shaped, in pairs) (1 mark).\n- **R - spiral, coiled or wavy** (accept *spirillum*, corkscrew-shaped, curved) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks, 1 mark each. Accept the everyday description or the scientific name; the candidate does not need both. Do not award a mark for naming a disease here - the question asks for shape.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Food poisoning** (from *Salmonella*, *Staphylococcus* or *Escherichia coli*).\n- **Gonorrhoea.**\n- **Meningitis.**\n- Also accept: **cholera, typhoid fever, tuberculosis, tetanus, whooping cough, syphilis, leprosy**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE correct bacterial diseases (3 marks). Refuse the mark for diseases that are not bacterial - malaria, measles, hepatitis B, HIV and AIDS, ringworm and athlete's foot are caused by protozoa, viruses or fungi.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Warmth** - the food is kept at room temperature, which is warm enough for the bacteria to grow.\n- **Moisture** - cooked food such as rice, stew or kenkey holds water, which the bacteria need.\n- **Food (nutrients)** - the bacteria feed on the starch and the protein in the meal.\n- Also accept: **enough time** for the numbers to double again and again, and the **food left uncovered** so that bacteria from the air, from dust and from flies settle on it.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE conditions (3 marks). 'Dirtiness' alone is too vague; it must be linked to germs reaching the food.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Food poisoning is an illness that follows the eating of food or the drinking of water that contains harmful bacteria or the poisons (toxins) that the bacteria have produced** (1 mark).\n- **Salmonella** (1 mark).\n- **Staphylococcus** (accept *Escherichia coli*, *Clostridium*, *Shigella*, *Campylobacter*) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks: 1 for the explanation, 1 for each of TWO named bacteria. The explanation must mention the germ or its poison in the food; 'food that is bad' alone earns nothing.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Vomiting.**\n- **Diarrhoea - frequent, watery stools.**\n- **Stomach (abdominal) pain or cramps.**\n- **Fever**, with chills.\n- Also accept: **nausea, headache, weakness and tiredness, dry mouth and great thirst**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any FOUR symptoms (4 marks). Mark only the first four given. 'Sickness' on its own is too vague.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **The food was cooked in the morning and served in the afternoon**, so it stood for several hours in a warm, moist state in which any surviving bacteria multiplied many times over.\n- **The pan was left open**, so dust, flies and unwashed hands could carry bacteria onto the food.\n- Also accept: **the food was not reheated thoroughly before it was served**, and **there was no cold store or refrigerator** to keep it safe.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO sound reasons (2 marks). The reason must explain how the bacteria got in or how they multiplied.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Wash the hands with soap under running water** before handling food, after using the toilet and after taking money, and keep the fingernails short.\n- **Cook the food thoroughly and serve it hot**, an"
