    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) In explanations, award method marks for correct reasoning even if the wording differs from the scheme. (4) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required.)*\n\n### Question 1 (8 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- The carbon cycle is the **continuous movement (circulation) of carbon** between the air, living things, the soil and **back to the air again**.\n- Carbon passes from carbon (IV) oxide in the air into green plants, then to animals, and back into the air, repeatedly.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct points (2 marks). Award 1 mark for ‘carbon moving round and round’ if the idea of movement between air and living things is present. Do NOT accept ‘the cycle of carbon’ alone.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Photosynthesis**\n- **Respiration**\n- **Burning** (combustion)\n- **Decay** (decomposition)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for all FOUR processes (4 marks). Accept ‘combustion’ for burning and ‘decomposition’ for decay. Do NOT accept ‘eating’ or ‘food chain’ as one of the four processes.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **same carbon atoms are used again and again** - carbon is not destroyed or used up at any stage.\n- The **processes repeat themselves over and over** in nature (carbon returns to the air and is taken up again).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct reasons (2 marks). Accept ‘it goes round and round’ only when the idea of repetition or of reuse is stated.*\n\n### Question 2 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Carbon (IV) oxide** (carbon dioxide)\n- **Water**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (2 marks). Do NOT accept oxygen or sunlight, which are needed but are not raw materials taken in by the plant.*\n\n**(b)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Oxygen**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for oxygen.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The plant uses the carbon (IV) oxide with water to **make food (carbohydrates)** (1 mark).\n- The **carbon becomes part of the plant's body** - it is stored in the food made, and oxygen is given out (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Accept ‘the carbon is trapped in the plant’ or ‘the carbon changes into food’. Do NOT accept ‘the carbon disappears’.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- The carbon taken from the air is stored in the **food made by the plant** (1 mark).\n- When the goat **eats the plant**, the food passes into the goat, so the carbon becomes part of the **goat's body** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Look for the idea that the carbon is in the food the plant made, and that eating transfers it to the animal.*\n\n**(e)** [1 marks]\n\n- It **produces food** for the plant and for animals (including humans), and it **gives out oxygen** for living things to breathe.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for either point, or for both.*\n\n**(f)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Little or no food** will be made (1 mark).\n- Reason: there is **no light** in the cupboard, and light is needed for photosynthesis (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark for the correct prediction and 1 mark for the reason. Accept ‘the plant will use up the food it has stored and may die’ as the reason if light is named.*\n\n### Question 3 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- Respiration is the process by which living things **obtain energy from food**.\n- It uses **oxygen** and gives out **carbon (IV) oxide** (and water) - it happens in all living cells, day and night.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct points (2 marks). Award 1 mark for ‘getting energy from food’ on its own; do NOT accept ‘breathing’ without the idea of energy from food.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- Photosynthesis **takes in carbon (IV) oxide** and gives out oxygen; respiration **takes in oxygen** and gives out carbon (IV) oxide.\n- Photosynthesis **needs light** and takes place only in **green plants**; respirati"
