    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award a method mark for correct working even where the final answer is wrong, and insist on the unit in the final answer. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Energy can neither be created nor destroyed** (1 mark);\n- it can only be **changed from one form into another**, so the **total energy before a change equals the total energy after it** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The first mark needs both 'not created' and 'not destroyed'; the second needs the change of form or the equality of the totals.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- Energy conservation means **using energy carefully and avoiding waste** in the home, the school and the workplace (1 mark);\n- so that **less fuel and electricity are used, costs are lower and the supply lasts longer** for the future (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Do NOT accept the law of conservation of energy as the answer here; the question asks for the everyday meaning.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Energy conversion** is the **changing of energy from one form into another**, for example electrical energy into light in a lamp (1 mark).\n- **Energy conservation** is **using energy carefully so that none is wasted**, for example switching the lamp off when it is not needed (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark for each side of the difference. A candidate who explains only one of the two terms scores 1 mark.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- It **puts energy into the form we actually need** - light for reading, heat for cooking, movement for transport and machines (1 mark).\n- It makes the **free natural energy of the Sun, the wind and flowing water useful** to people (1 mark).\n- It **powers industry, hospitals, schools and communication**, so it supports work, education, health and the economy (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE sound points (3 marks). This answers the curriculum exemplar on classifying the importance of energy conversion in daily life.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [1 marks]\n\n- The **air conditioner**, which used **120 kWh**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- **120 + 90 + 45 + 30 + 15** (1 mark)\n- **= 300 kWh** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 mark for the correct addition of all five values and 1 mark for the answer with the unit kWh.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **(120 ÷ 300) × 100** (1 mark)\n- **= 40%** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 mark for the correct method and 1 mark for the answer. Follow through from a wrong total in (b) - award the method mark.*\n\n**(d)** [1 marks]\n\n- **90 − 15 = 75 kWh**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. The unit must be given.*\n\n**(e)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Use the air conditioner for fewer hours, or use a fan instead**, and keep the doors and windows closed while it works.\n- **Fit energy-saving (LED) bulbs and switch off lights that are not needed**, using daylight where possible.\n- **Iron many clothes at one time**, **avoid opening the refrigerator too often**, and **switch off the television when nobody is watching**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE practical ways (3 marks). The answer should relate to the appliances shown in Fig. 1.*\n\n**(f)** [2 marks]\n\n- Lighting used **45 kWh**, so the cost is **45 × GH¢1.50** (1 mark)\n- **= GH¢67.50** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 mark for reading 45 kWh from the chart and multiplying, 1 mark for the answer in cedis. A candidate who uses the wrong bar but multiplies correctly earns the method mark only.*\n\n**(g)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Less electricity used means less fuel has to be burnt at the power station**, so **fewer harmful gases are released into the air** and fewer natural resources are used "
