    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) For procedures, investigations and design tasks, award method marks. (4) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required.)*\n\n### Question 1 (8 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- A seed bed is a **piece of land that has been prepared (cleared, dug, levelled)** for planting.\n- The soil in it is made **loose and fine** so that seeds can germinate and the young roots can grow easily.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct points (2 marks). Award 1 mark for ‘land prepared for planting’ alone. Do NOT accept ‘a place where seeds are stored’.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Flat bed**\n- **Raised bed**\n- **Sunken bed**\n- (Also accept: **nursery bed**, **ridges**, **mounds**)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE correct types (3 marks). Accept ridges and mounds, which are used in many farming communities in Ghana.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- To **loosen the soil** so that seeds germinate easily and roots can spread.\n- To **remove weeds, stumps and stones** that would compete with the crops or obstruct the work.\n- To **improve drainage and the movement of air** in the soil, and to **mix in manure** so the soil is more fertile.\n- To make **planting, weeding and harvesting easier** for the farmer.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE correct reasons (3 marks). Do NOT accept ‘to make the farm beautiful’.*\n\n### Question 2 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **flat bed** (the soil is level with the surrounding ground)\n- B - **raised bed** (the soil is heaped above the surrounding ground)\n- C - **sunken bed** (the soil is dug below the surrounding ground)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (3 marks). The name must be matched to the correct letter. Accept ‘flat seed bed’, ‘raised seed bed’ and ‘sunken seed bed’.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- **B - the raised bed** (1 mark).\n- Because the bed stands above the ground, **excess water drains away** and the roots **get air instead of rotting** in the water (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 mark for the correct bed and 1 mark for the reason. If the bed named is wrong, no marks are awarded even if the reason is sound.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **C - the sunken bed** (1 mark).\n- Because it lies below the ground it **holds water (and moisture) around the roots for a longer time**, which the crops need in dry weather (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 mark for the correct bed and 1 mark for the reason. Accept ‘it conserves moisture’ for the reason mark.*\n\n**(d)** [1 marks]\n\n- It is **easy and quick to prepare** (little soil has to be moved), so a large area can be planted; it suits most crops on **well-drained land**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for any one correct advantage.*\n\n**(e)** [2 marks]\n\n- The soil of **B is heaped above the ground** while the soil of **C is dug below the ground**.\n- **B drains water away** from the crops, whereas **C holds water** around the crops.\n- **B is used in wet or waterlogged places**, while **C is used in dry places**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct differences (2 marks). The answer must compare the two beds; a statement about only one of them earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 3 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) Cassava - **stem cuttings**\n- (ii) Maize - **seeds** (grains)\n- (iii) Plantain - **suckers**\n- (iv) Yam - **setts** (pieces cut from the tuber)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). Accept ‘stem’ for cassava, ‘grains’ for maize and ‘tuber pieces’ for yam. Do NOT accept ‘seeds’ for cassava, plantain or yam.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- Transplanting is the **lifting of young seedlings from a nursery bed** (or seed box) (1 mark)\n- and **planting them in the main field** or in their permanent position (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Award 1 mark only for ‘moving"
