    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) In the flow chart, award marks for the correct stages in the correct order; the shape of the boxes does not matter. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- Electricity transmission is the **carrying of electrical energy from the power station where it is generated** (1 mark)\n- **through cables, pylons, transformers and substations to the places where it is used** - homes, schools and factories (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Step-up transformer** - it **raises the voltage** of the electricity leaving the power station (1 mark) **before it is sent along the transmission lines**, so that less energy is lost on the way (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Pylon** - the **tall steel tower that carries the high-voltage cables high above the ground**, out of reach (1 mark).\n- (iii) **Insulator** - it **holds the cable away from the metal pylon** so that the current cannot pass into the tower and down to earth (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 4 marks as shown. In (i), 'it changes the voltage' earns 1 mark only; the direction of the change must be stated.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- The cables have **resistance**, so some electrical energy is always **turned into heat in them** as the current flows (1 mark).\n- For the same amount of power, a **higher voltage means a smaller current** flowing in the cables (1 mark).\n- The heat lost depends on the size of the current, so the **smaller current wastes far less energy** over the long distance (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks, 1 mark each. No formula is required; award full marks for the reasoning 'higher voltage - smaller current - less heat lost'.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) J - the **step-up transformer** at the power station (1 mark)\n- (ii) K - the **step-down transformer at the substation** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (2 marks). The side notes in Fig. 1 say that the voltage is raised at J and lowered at K; accept 'substation' for K only where the lowering of the voltage is also mentioned.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- Its **voltage is raised at the power station and lowered again at the substation**.\n- It **travels long distances along cables carried on pylons and is then shared out along the distribution lines** (accept: some of the energy is lost as heat in the cables).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct points (2 marks).*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The transmission voltage is **far too high for use in houses**: it would be **extremely dangerous and would destroy the appliances** (1 mark).\n- It must be **brought down to the value that lamps, fans and other appliances are made to work on** (about 230 V in Ghana) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Accept the safety point or the appliance point for either mark.*\n\n**(d)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Four boxes drawn and joined by arrows pointing in one direction** (1 mark).\n- **Box 1: the power station**, where the electricity is generated (1 mark).\n- **Box 2 and Box 3: the step-up transformer with the transmission lines, and the substation with its step-down transformer** (1 mark).\n- **Box 4: the school (the consumer)**, where the electricity is used (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 4 marks as shown. This is the curriculum exemplar that asks for a flow chart from the point of generation to the point of consumption. A chart whose arrows run the wrong way, or whose stages are out of order, loses the first mark.*\n\n**(e)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Never touch or go near a fallen cable; keep others away and report it at once to the electricity company.**\n- **Do not fly kites, climb trees or raise ladders and long metal objects near the lines**, and **do not build "
