    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) An energy change must show both the starting form and the final form; a single form earns no mark. (3) Accept 'mechanical energy' for 'kinetic energy' where the body is clearly moving. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- Energy conversion is the **changing of energy from one form into another form** (1 mark),\n- for example **electrical energy changing into heat energy in a kettle**; the energy is **not destroyed - it only changes its form** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The second mark may be earned either by a correct example or by the point that the energy is not destroyed.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Electrical energy to heat energy** (1 mark)\n- (ii) **Chemical energy to electrical energy to light energy** (accept chemical to light) (1 mark)\n- (iii) **Electrical energy to sound energy** (1 mark)\n- (iv) **Light (solar) energy to chemical energy** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). Both the starting form and the final form must be given; a single form earns no mark. In (ii) accept the two-step or the three-step chain.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Heat energy** - from the cooking stove or the coal pot.\n- **Light energy** - from the electric lamp or the lantern.\n- **Sound energy** - from the radio or the television (accept electrical energy at the socket, or chemical energy in food and dry cells).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for each correct pairing of a form of energy with a place where it is used or produced (3 marks). A form named without a place, or a place without a form, earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Potential energy** (gravitational potential energy), because the water is stored at a high level\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Do NOT accept kinetic energy; the stored water is not yet moving.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- The **dam wall holds back the river**, so that a large volume of water is **stored at a high level** in the reservoir (1 mark).\n- The water is released and **rushes down the penstock**, so its potential energy changes into **kinetic energy** (1 mark).\n- The fast-moving water **strikes the blades of the turbine and makes it spin** (1 mark).\n- The spinning turbine **drives the generator through the shaft**, and the generator **produces electricity**, which is carried away by the transmission lines (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for each correct step in the right order (4 marks). This is the curriculum exemplar on the processes a dammed river goes through to produce electricity. A candidate who merely writes 'water turns turbine, turbine turns generator' earns a maximum of 2 marks.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) **Kinetic energy of the moving water to mechanical (kinetic) energy of the turbine** (1 mark)\n- (ii) **Mechanical (kinetic) energy to electrical energy** (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (2 marks). Both forms must be stated in each case.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **higher the water is stored above the turbine, the greater its potential energy** (1 mark).\n- As it falls, this becomes a **greater kinetic energy**, so the water **strikes the turbine with greater force and more electricity is produced** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The link between the height, the energy and the output must be made; 'so that the water can flow' alone earns 1 mark at most.*\n\n**(e)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **water is renewable and free** once the dam has been built, so the **running cost is low** and no fuel has to be bought.\n- **No fuel is burnt**, so there is **no smoke or air pollution** (accept: the reservoir also provides fish, water for irrigation and transport).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO (2 marks).*\n\n**(f)** [1 marks]\n\n- **People living in the valley lose thei"
