    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) Where a candidate is asked to state a number of points, mark only the first points up to the number asked for. (4) Credit local examples drawn from the candidate's own community. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- A farming system is the **particular way a farmer combines crops, animals and land** on his holding (1 mark),\n- using his **labour, capital and other resources to produce food and other farm products** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The combination of the components must appear for the first mark. A list of crops alone earns no mark.*\n\n**(b)** [6 marks]\n\n- (i) **Mixed farming** - the growing of crops and the keeping of farm animals **together on the same holding**, for example maize with cattle (2 marks)\n- (ii) **Mono-cropping** - the growing of **only one kind of crop on the same piece of land season after season**, for example maize every year (2 marks)\n- (iii) **Crop rotation** - the growing of **different crops one after the other on the same piece of land in a planned order**, so that the same crop does not return to the land every season (2 marks)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks for each term (6 marks): 1 mark for the idea and 1 mark for a correct example or for the point that distinguishes it. Candidates commonly confuse mixed farming with mixed cropping - mixed cropping has no animals in it.*\n\n**(c)** [1 marks]\n\n- **The climate and rainfall of the area** (accept the type of soil, the size of land available, the capital and labour the farmer has, the nearby market, or the custom and experience of the community).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for any one sound reason.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Mixed farming** (crops and animals kept together on one holding)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Do NOT accept ‘mixed cropping’, which does not include animals.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **cattle manure is spread on the maize field**, adding nutrients and improving the soil.\n- The **poultry droppings are used as manure on the vegetable plot** (accept: the oxen plough the fields).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO (2 marks). Both answers must be taken from the arrows in Fig. 1.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **maize stalks and husks are fed to the cattle**, and the **maize grain is used as poultry feed**.\n- The **spoilt vegetable leaves are fed to the fowls**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO feed links shown in Fig. 1 (2 marks).*\n\n**(d)** [1 marks]\n\n- The **oxen plough both fields**, providing draught power that would otherwise cost money to hire.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. The ploughing is stated beneath the diagram; manure is NOT accepted here, as the question excludes it.*\n\n**(e)** [3 marks]\n\n- The **farm wastes are used on the farm itself**, so he **spends less money on fertiliser and on feed** (1 mark).\n- He has **several products - maize, vegetables, milk, eggs - and so a steadier income throughout the year** (1 mark).\n- The **risk is spread**: if one enterprise fails, the others still bring in money; and the **land and labour are fully used** while the soil is kept fertile (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE sound advantages (3 marks). Credit answers that are clearly drawn from Fig. 1.*\n\n**(f)** [2 marks]\n\n- It needs **more skill, labour and capital**, and the farmer's **attention is divided** between the crops and the animals.\n- **Animals may stray and damage the crops**, and **both crops and animals bring pests and diseases** that cost money to control (accept: housing, water and veterinary care are extra costs).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO (2 marks). ‘It is difficult’ on its own earns no mark; the difficulty must be explained.*\n\n**(g)** [1 marks]\n\n- **The waste from one part of the farm becomes a resource for anoth"
