    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) For descriptions, demonstrations and design tasks, award method marks. (4) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Crown**\n- **Neck**\n- **Root**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (3 marks). These are the three regions named in the curriculum. Do NOT accept enamel, dentine and pulp - those are the layers, not the regions.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) Crown - the part **above the gum** that **bites, tears or grinds the food**\n- (ii) Root - **fixes and holds the tooth firmly** in the socket of the jaw bone\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (2 marks). Accept ‘the part used for chewing’ for the crown and ‘anchors the tooth’ for the root.*\n\n**(c)** [1 marks]\n\n- **32**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Do NOT accept 20, which is the number of milk teeth.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- Milk teeth are the **first set and are 20 in number**, while permanent teeth are the **second set and are 32 in number**.\n- Milk teeth are **smaller and fall out (are replaced)**, while permanent teeth are **larger and stronger and are not replaced** when they are lost.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct differences (2 marks). The answer must compare the two sets; a statement about only one of them earns no mark.*\n\n**(e)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Enamel**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Do NOT accept ‘bone’ or ‘dentine’.*\n\n### Question 2 (11 marks)\n\n**(a)** [6 marks]\n\n- I - **enamel**\n- II - **dentine**\n- III - **pulp cavity**\n- IV - **gum**\n- V - **cement**\n- VI - **nerve and blood vessels**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (6 marks). The name must be matched to the correct number. Accept ‘pulp’ for III, ‘gums’ for IV and ‘cementum’ for V. For VI accept either ‘nerve’ or ‘blood vessels’ alone.*\n\n**(b)** [1 marks]\n\n- The enamel is the **hard outer covering that protects the crown** of the tooth from wear, from damage during chewing and from decay.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for protection or hardness of the crown.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- It contains the **nerves**, which make the tooth **sensitive to pain, heat and cold** (1 mark).\n- It contains the **blood vessels**, which **supply food and oxygen** to the living cells of the tooth and keep it alive (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. Both the nerve function and the blood supply must appear for full marks.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **pulp cavity contains the nerves** of the tooth, while the enamel and dentine outside it have no nerves (1 mark).\n- When the decay reaches the pulp the **nerves are exposed and irritated** by food, acid, heat and cold, and the pulp may become infected, so **severe pain (toothache) is felt** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The first mark is for locating the nerves in the pulp; the second is for the exposure or irritation of those nerves.*\n\n### Question 3 (11 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- W - **incisor**\n- X - **canine**\n- Y - **premolar**\n- Z - **molar**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). The name must be matched to the correct letter. Accept ‘eye tooth’ for the canine.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) W (incisor) - **cuts and bites** off pieces of food\n- (ii) Z (molar) - **grinds and chews** the food into small pieces\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (2 marks). Do NOT accept ‘chewing’ for the incisor or ‘cutting’ for the molar.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **X is pointed (has one sharp cusp)** while **Z is broad with a large, ridged grinding surface**.\n- **X is used for tearing** food, while **Z is used for grinding and chewing** it.\n- **X has one root** and stands **near the front** of the mouth, while **Z has two or more roots** and stands **at the back**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct differences (2 marks). The answer must comp"
