    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) In explanations, award method marks for correct reasoning even if the wording differs from the scheme. (4) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required.)*\n\n### Question 1 (8 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- A cell is the **basic (smallest) unit of all living things**.\n- **All living things are made up of one or more cells.**\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct points (2 marks). Award 1 mark for ‘the smallest part of a living thing’ if the idea of a unit of life is present. Do NOT accept ‘a small thing’ on its own.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) Nucleus - **controls all the activities of the cell** (and contains the genetic material).\n- (ii) Cell membrane - **controls the movement of substances into and out of the cell**.\n- (iii) Cytoplasm - the **living material** in which the other organelles are found; most cell activities take place there.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (3 marks). The function must be matched to the correct part; a correct statement attached to the wrong part earns no mark.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **Chloroplast** (accept ‘chloroplasts’)\n- (ii) **Cell wall**\n- (iii) **Vacuole** (accept ‘large central vacuole’)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (3 marks). Do NOT accept ‘cell membrane’ for (ii) or ‘cytoplasm’ for (iii).*\n\n### Question 2 (8 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Chloroplasts**\n- **Cell wall**\n- **Large central vacuole** (accept ‘large vacuole’)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE (3 marks). Accept ‘plastids’ for chloroplasts. Do NOT accept nucleus, cytoplasm or cell membrane - these are found in both cells.*\n\n**(b)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Centriole** (accept ‘small vacuoles’ or ‘centrosome’)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark. Do NOT accept ‘mitochondria’, which are found in both cells.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- Both have a **cell membrane** and **cytoplasm**.\n- Both have a **nucleus** and other organelles such as mitochondria and ribosomes.\n- Both are **living** and carry out life processes such as respiration.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct similarities (2 marks). The similarity must be true of BOTH cells; a difference given as a similarity earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- It **stores cell sap** (water with dissolved sugars and salts).\n- It helps to **keep the cell firm (turgid)** and therefore gives the plant support.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO correct functions (2 marks). Do NOT accept ‘it makes food’ - that is the work of chloroplasts.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- I - **cell wall**\n- II - **cell membrane**\n- III - **cytoplasm** (accept ‘cytoplasm with ribosomes’)\n- IV - **nuclear material** (accept ‘DNA’, ‘loop of DNA’, ‘genetic material’)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). The label must be matched to the correct part. Do NOT accept ‘nucleus’ for IV - a prokaryotic cell has no true nucleus.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- I (cell wall): **gives the cell its fixed shape** and **protects** the cell (1 mark).\n- II (cell membrane): **controls the movement of substances** into and out of the cell (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The function must be matched to the correct label.*\n\n**(c)** [1 marks]\n\n- **Prokaryotic organisms** (accept ‘prokaryotes’)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for the correct group.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- A bacterial cell has **no true nucleus** - its genetic material lies loose in the cytoplasm.\n- It has **no membrane-bound organelles** such as mitochondria and chloroplasts.\n- It has **no chloroplasts and no large central vacuole**, and some bacteria have a capsule and flagellum.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE correct differences (3 marks). Accept ‘it is much smaller’ or ‘it is unicellular’ paired with a structural difference, but do "
