    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (3) In the tables, award the marks entry by entry; a candidate who writes the answers as a list instead of a table still earns the content marks. (4) Insist on the distinction between heat (energy, in joules) and temperature (degree of hotness, in kelvin or degrees Celsius). (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) A **renewable source of energy is one that is replaced by nature as fast as it is used, so it does not run out** (1 mark); examples are **solar, wind, hydro and biogas** (1 mark).\n- (ii) A **non-renewable source took millions of years to form and is being used up far faster than it can be replaced** (1 mark); examples are **coal, crude oil and natural gas** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 4 marks: 1 mark for each definition and 1 mark for at least one correct example in each case.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Renewable sources:** wind, hydro, biogas\n- **Non-renewable sources:** coal, crude oil, natural gas\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks: award 1 mark for each pair of correct entries (6 correct entries = 3 marks; 4 correct = 2 marks; 2 correct = 1 mark). A candidate who writes the answers as two lists instead of a table still earns the marks. The list of sources is taken from the curriculum exemplar.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- They are **replaced by nature and will not run out**, while fossil fuels will one day be finished.\n- They **do not pollute the air with smoke and harmful gases**, and the source itself is **free**, so the running cost is low (accept: they can serve villages far from the national grid).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO (2 marks).*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) P - the **inlet (mixing) tank**, through which the dung and water are fed into the plant (1 mark)\n- (ii) Q - the **digester (digestion tank)**, in which the dung and water are broken down (1 mark)\n- (iii) R - the **gas outlet pipe**, which carries the gas to the kitchen (1 mark)\n- (iv) S - the **outlet (slurry outlet pipe and pit)**, through which the used slurry leaves the plant (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (4 marks). Accept any wording that shows the correct function - for example 'feeding tank' for P and 'gas pipe' for R.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Biogas** (mainly **methane**) (1 mark)\n- Use: it is **burnt in the school kitchen for cooking** (accept: for lighting the lamps) (1 mark)\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Animal dung is mixed with water and fed in through the inlet tank** (1 mark).\n- The mixture is held in the **closed digester, from which air is kept out** (1 mark).\n- **Bacteria break down the dung**, and the **gas formed collects above the slurry and is piped off** through R (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks, 1 mark each. The exclusion of air or the action of bacteria must appear for the second or third mark; an answer that says only 'the dung rots' earns 1 mark.*\n\n**(d)** [1 marks]\n\n- The **slurry is used as manure on the school farm** (accept: the compound is kept clean and flies are reduced; the school saves the money spent on firewood, charcoal or LPG; fewer trees are cut for firewood).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark for any one correct benefit other than the gas itself.*\n\n**(e)** [2 marks]\n\n- The **dung and organic waste are produced every day** for as long as the animals are kept and the farm is worked (1 mark).\n- The source is therefore **replaced as fast as it is used and cannot be used up**, which is what makes a source renewable (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. The idea of continuous replacement must appear.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **Solar:** there is **no sunshine at night and less on cloudy days**, so storage is needed; the **panels and batteries are costly to buy and to replace**, and **dust on t"
