    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award a method mark for correct working even where the final answer is wrong, and insist on the unit cm³ where a volume is asked for. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed. (6) Where a candidate names a rock or a soil found in his or her own district that the scheme does not list, award the mark if the description fits.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Aim: to find out how much water each of the three soils holds back, or lets through** (1 mark).\n- **The property compared is the water-retaining ability (water retention) of the soils**, which is the other side of how freely they drain (1 mark).\n- Accept also: *to compare the drainage of the three soils*.\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 each. 'To test the soils' is too vague. Accept either retention or drainage for the second mark, since each implies the other.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Soil A: 100 − 42 = 58 cm³** (1 mark).\n- **Soil B: 100 − 25 = 75 cm³** (1 mark).\n- **Soil C: 100 − 8 = 92 cm³** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 3 marks, 1 each. The working 100 minus the volume collected must be seen or implied. Insist on cm³ at least once; do not penalise the missing unit three times over.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **A is the sandy soil**: it let the most water through (42 cm³), because its particles are large and the spaces between them are wide, so water drains freely (1 mark).\n- **C is the clay soil**: it let only 8 cm³ through and held back 92 cm³, because its particles are very fine and tightly packed, so the water is held in the tiny spaces (1 mark).\n- It follows that **B is the loamy soil**, which lies between the two.\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks: 1 for each soil correctly identified WITH a reason drawn from the particle size or the spaces. A bare letter with no reason earns nothing.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The same mass (or volume) of soil in each funnel.**\n- **The same volume of water poured into each funnel** - here 100 cm³.\n- **The same funnels, and the same filter paper or cotton wool** at the neck of each.\n- Also accept: **all three soils dried in the same way before the test**; **the water poured at the same rate**; **the same time allowed for the water to drain before reading**; **the soils packed to the same tightness**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE controlled variables (3 marks). The answer must be something that is KEPT THE SAME; naming the soil type, which is the thing being changed, earns nothing.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Texture** - how coarse or fine the soil feels, which depends on the size of its particles.\n- **Colour** - dark brown or black soils are usually rich in humus, while pale or red soils are usually poorer in it.\n- **Water-retaining ability and drainage** - how much water the soil holds and how fast the rest passes through.\n- Also accept: **air content (porosity)**; **structure, that is the way the particles cling together into crumbs**; **the amount of humus**.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE physical properties (3 marks). The fertility or the pH of a soil is not a physical property and earns nothing.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Size of the particles** - sandy: **large (coarse)**; clay: **very small (fine)** (1 mark for the row).\n- **Drainage of water** - sandy: **very fast, free drainage**; clay: **very slow, often waterlogged** (1 mark).\n- **Amount of water retained** - sandy: **little**; clay: **a great deal** (1 mark).\n- **Ease of working with a hoe** - sandy: **light and easy to work**; clay: **heavy and hard to work - sticky when wet, hard and cracked when dry** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 4 marks, 1 for each complete row. Both cells of a row must be correct for the mark; a row with o"
