    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) In calculations, award a method mark for correct working even where the final answer is wrong, and insist on the per cent sign where a percentage is asked for. (3) Award marks for correct points, not for repeating the question. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (9 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Waste** is **any material that is no longer wanted or useful to the person or place that produced it** (1 mark).\n- **Waste management** is the **collection, sorting, treatment and safe disposal of waste**, including reducing, reusing and recycling it (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- **Solid waste** - plastic bottles, sachet-water bags, broken bottles, sweepings.\n- **Liquid waste** - bath water, water from the kitchen sink, waste water from a factory.\n- **Gaseous waste** - smoke from a chimney, exhaust fumes from a vehicle.\n- **Medical waste** - used syringes, bandages and expired drugs (accept domestic, agricultural, industrial or electronic waste with a correct example).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any FOUR types, each with a correct example (4 marks). A type named with no example, or with a wrong example, scores nothing for that item. Candidates may use either classification - by state (solid, liquid, gaseous) or by source (domestic, industrial, agricultural, medical, electronic) - or a mixture of the two.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- To **keep the community clean and free from offensive smells**, and to **prevent diseases** such as cholera, typhoid and malaria.\n- To **stop gutters from choking and causing floods**, and to **protect the soil, the streams and the air from pollution**.\n- To **recover useful materials and manure** from the waste, and to keep the community attractive to visitors and to business.\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any THREE sound reasons (3 marks).*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [5 marks]\n\n- **A - reduce** (prevention: avoid producing the waste in the first place) (1 mark).\n- **B - reuse** (1 mark).\n- **C - recycle** (1 mark).\n- **D - recover** - composting, or recovering energy from the waste (1 mark).\n- **E - disposal** - landfill or incineration (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (5 marks). The order must be as shown. Accept 'refuse' or 'prevent' for A, 'composting' for D and 'dumping in a sanitary landfill' for E.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Waste that is never produced does not have to be collected, treated or disposed of at all**, so no land, fuel or money is spent on it and it cannot pollute the environment (1 mark).\n- It also **saves the raw materials and the energy** that would have been used to make the goods in the first place (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 2 marks, 1 mark each. A candidate who merely writes 'because it is at the top' scores 0.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **A (reduce):** **carry a basket to the market instead of collecting plastic bags**, or write and photocopy on both sides of the paper (1 mark).\n- **B (reuse):** **wash a glass bottle and use it again to store drinking water**, or use empty tins as pots for seedlings in the school garden (1 mark).\n- **C (recycle):** **collect plastic bottles and sachet bags and send them to a recycling factory** to be made into new plastic items (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each (3 marks). The example must match the level: a candidate who gives recycling as an example of reuse scores 0 for that mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Nothing useful is recovered** - the materials, the plant nutrients and the energy in the waste are all lost (1 mark).\n- It **takes up land**, and dumps and open burning **pollute the soil, the water and the air and breed flies, rats and mosquitoes** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 1 mark each for any TWO sound reasons (2 marks).*\n\n### Question 3 "
